<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Wilusa</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.) · capital Wilusa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wilusa, a kingdom of north-western Anatolia in Hittite texts, widely identified with Homer's Troy and its city of Ilios.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wilusa appears in Hittite records from the 15th century BCE, first as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/assuwa">Assuwa</a> league. Most scholars identify it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> at Hisarlik, guarding the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Dardanelles</a>, whose sixth and seventh cities were large walled citadels with a lower town.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>. Around 1280 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaksandu">Alaksandu</a> of Wilusa, whose name resembles Alexandros, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the treaty invokes a god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appaliuna">Appaliuna</a>. Later letters show the Mycenaean king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a> contesting control of Wilusa and the deposition of a king named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walmu">Walmu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> VIIa was destroyed by fire around 1180 BCE, at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, and Wilusa vanishes from written sources. Greek epic transformed its memory into the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wilusa.jpg" alt="Wilusa" width="320" height="197"><figcaption>Image: Al-qamar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hethiter.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wilusa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kukkunni, Alaksandu, Walmu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anatolian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Troy, Wilion</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1290&amp;focus=wilusa" aria-label="Wilusa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5558 756 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wilusa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5852 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5878 1012h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5878 1012h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=-1290&amp;focus=wilusa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

